(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["prefix",{"l:prefix/prefix-sums-and-half-open-ranges":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Store \u003ccode>pre[i]\u003c/code> as the sum of the first i elements, so \u003ccode>pre[0] = 0\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pre\u003c/code> has n + 1 entries. The sum of the half-open slice \u003ccode>a[l:r]\u003c/code> is then \u003ccode>pre[r] - pre[l]\u003c/code>: one subtraction, whatever the length. You pay O(n) once to build the table and O(1) per query, as long as the data does not change.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Invariant:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>pre[i] == sum(a[:i])\u003c/code>. It holds for i = 0 (the empty sum) and \u003ccode>pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + a[i]\u003c/code> keeps it. Subtracting two prefixes cancels their shared part, so \u003ccode>pre[r] - pre[l]\u003c/code> is exactly \u003ccode>a[l] + ... + a[r - 1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why half-open:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>a[l:r]\u003c/code> matches Python slicing and has length \u003ccode>r - l\u003c/code>, and the leading zero removes the special case \u003ccode>l == 0\u003c/code>. An inclusive question \u003ccode>[l, r]\u003c/code> becomes \u003ccode>pre[r + 1] - pre[l]\u003c/code>; convert once at the edge of your code, never in the middle.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fence posts:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>pre[i]\u003c/code> sits on the post just before \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code>, which is why there is one more post than there are panels.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Python costs:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>list(accumulate(a, initial=0))\u003c/code> runs the loop in C, several times faster than a Python \u003ccode>for\u003c/code> loop. Each prefix is its own int object (28 bytes or more, plus an 8-byte list slot); for millions of numbers \u003ccode>numpy.cumsum\u003c/code> stores packed 8-byte integers instead. Python ints never overflow; NumPy's int64 and Java's or C++'s fixed-width integers do, silently.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The hidden O(n):\u003c/b> \u003ccode>sum(a[l:r])\u003c/code> inside a loop copies the slice and adds it up every time. A loop of q such queries is O(nq), exactly the cost the table removes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Static data only:\u003c/b> changing \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> invalidates every entry from \u003ccode>pre[i + 1]\u003c/code> on, an O(n) rewrite. When updates and queries interleave, a Fenwick tree keeps both at O(log n); the range-query module builds one.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from itertools import accumulate\n\n\ndef build_prefix(a):\n    \"\"\"pre[i] = a[0] + ... + a[i-1], so pre[0] = 0 and len(pre) = len(a) + 1.\"\"\"\n    pre = [0] * (len(a) + 1)\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + x\n    return pre\n\n\ndef range_sum(pre, l, r):\n    \"\"\"Sum of a[l:r] (half-open), O(1).\"\"\"\n    return pre[r] - pre[l]\n\n\n# The same table in one line, with the loop running in C:\n# pre = list(accumulate(a, initial=0))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start with \u003ccode>pre[0] = 0\u003c/code>, the sum of the first zero elements; \u003ccode>pre\u003c/code> will have one more entry than \u003ccode>a\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7\na         3   1   4   1   5   9   2\npre       \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + a[i]\u003c/code>: 0 + 3 = 3, then 3 + 1 = 4, then 4 + 4 = 8\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7\na         3   1   4   1   5   9   2\npre       0   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Carry on to the end: \u003ccode>pre[7] = 25\u003c/code> is the total. Invariant: \u003ccode>pre[i]\u003c/code> is the sum of the \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> values in columns before i\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7\na         3   1   4   1   5   9   2\npre       0   3   4   8   \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>14\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>23\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>25\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Query \u003ccode>sum(a[2:5])\u003c/code>, the 4, 1 and 5: it is everything before column 5 minus everything before column 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6   7\na         3   1   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   9   2\npre       0   3   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   8   9  \u003cmark>14\u003c/mark>  23  25\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">\u003ccode>pre[5] - pre[2] = 14 - 4 = 10\u003c/code>: two lookups, whatever the length of the range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>sum(a[2:5]) = 14 - 4 = 10 = 4 + 1 + 5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Mixed conventions:\u003c/b> answering an inclusive \u003ccode>[l, r]\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>pre[r] - pre[l]\u003c/code> silently drops \u003ccode>a[r]\u003c/code>. Write the convention in the docstring.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No leading zero:\u003c/b> building \u003ccode>pre[i] = sum(a[:i + 1])\u003c/code> forces an \u003ccode>if l == 0\u003c/code> branch and invites off-by-one errors.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Stale tables:\u003c/b> mutating \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> after building \u003ccode>pre\u003c/code> does not update it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Floats:\u003c/b> differences of large running totals lose precision (cancellation). Keep integers (cents, counts), or use \u003ccode>math.fsum\u003c/code> when exactness matters.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overflow elsewhere:\u003c/b> 2 x 10^5 values up to 10^9 sum to 2 x 10^14, beyond a 32-bit \u003ccode>int\u003c/code>; use \u003ccode>long long\u003c/code> in C++ and \u003ccode>long\u003c/code> in Java.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">For \u003ccode>a = [2, -1, 3, 0, 4]\u003c/code>, what is \u003ccode>pre\u003c/code>, and which two entries give the sum of \u003ccode>a[1:4]\u003c/code>?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>pre = [0, 2, 1, 4, 4, 8]\u003c/code>. The sum of \u003ccode>a[1:4]\u003c/code> (that is -1, 3 and 0) is \u003ccode>pre[4] - pre[1] = 4 - 2 = 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to turn any static range-sum question into two lookups, and why the leading zero and half-open ranges remove the off-by-one errors.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/counting-subarrays-with-a-prefix-map":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The subarray \u003ccode>a[j:i]\u003c/code> sums to k exactly when \u003ccode>pre[i] - pre[j] == k\u003c/code>, that is when some \u003cb>earlier prefix equals \u003ccode>cur - k\u003c/code>\u003c/b>. Scan once with a running sum \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code> and a map from every prefix value seen so far to how often it occurred: add \u003ccode>seen[cur - k]\u003c/code> to the answer, then record \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code>. That is O(n) time and O(n) space, and negative values change nothing.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why not a sliding window:\u003c/b> a window shrinks when its sum is too big, which assumes that adding an element never lowers the sum. One negative value breaks that assumption and the window skips valid answers. Prefix differences do not care about signs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Look up, then record:\u003c/b> after adding \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code> is \u003ccode>pre[i + 1]\u003c/code> and the map holds \u003ccode>pre[0..i]\u003c/code>, so every match is a non-empty subarray ending at i. Recording \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code> first lets it match itself: an empty subarray, which sums to 0 and corrupts the answer when k = 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Seed \u003ccode>{0: 1}\u003c/code>:\u003c/b> the empty prefix \u003ccode>pre[0] = 0\u003c/code> is what lets subarrays starting at index 0 be counted.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counter, defaultdict or dict.get:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>Counter\u003c/code> returns 0 for a missing key \u003ci>without inserting it\u003c/i>; \u003ccode>defaultdict(int)\u003c/code> inserts on every read, so failed lookups grow the map; \u003ccode>seen.get(x, 0)\u003c/code> works on a plain dict.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>One scan, several questions:\u003c/b> to count, map prefixes to counts; for the longest, map each prefix to its \u003ci>first\u003c/i> index (seed \u003ccode>{0: -1}\u003c/code>, never overwrite); for the shortest, keep the \u003ci>last\u003c/i> index (overwrite); to test existence, a set is enough.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cost:\u003c/b> n lookups and n inserts at expected O(1) each (the hashing module covers why), plus O(n) space for the distinct prefixes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Two dimensions:\u003c/b> fix a band of rows, collapse each column of the band to its sum, and run this count on that 1D array (the 2D recap shows the band trick).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\n\ndef count_sum_k(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Number of subarrays with sum exactly k, O(n).\"\"\"\n    seen = Counter({0: 1})         # the empty prefix, before a[0]\n    cur = count = 0\n    for x in a:\n        cur += x\n        count += seen[cur - k]     # earlier prefixes p with cur - p == k\n        seen[cur] += 1\n    return count\n\n\ndef longest_sum_k(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Length of the longest subarray with sum exactly k (0 if none), O(n).\"\"\"\n    first = {0: -1}                # prefix value -&gt; first index where it occurred\n    cur = best = 0\n    for i, x in enumerate(a):\n        cur += x\n        if cur - k in first:\n            best = max(best, i - first[cur - k])\n        first.setdefault(cur, i)   # keep the earliest index only\n    return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the subarrays of \u003ccode>[3, 4, -7, 3, 1, 3]\u003c/code> that sum to k = 7. Watch prefix 0 appear twice: that duplicate is why the map stores counts, not just presence.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Seed the map with the empty prefix: \u003ccode>seen = {0: 1}\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>cur = 0\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>count = 0\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  -7   3   1   3\nseen  {0: 1}\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 0: \u003ccode>cur = 3\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>cur - k = -4\u003c/code>: not seen, so no subarray ends here; then record 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   4  -7   3   1   3\ncur   3    need -4    matches 0\nseen  {0: 1, 3: 1}\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 1: \u003ccode>cur = 7\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>0\u003c/code>: seen once (the empty prefix), so \u003ccode>a[0:2] = [3, 4]\u003c/code> sums to 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>  -7   3   1   3\ncur   7    need 0    matches 1\nseen  {0: 1, 3: 1, 7: 1}\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 2: \u003ccode>cur = 0\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>cur - k = -7\u003c/code>: not seen, so no subarray ends here; then record 0, which is now in the map twice\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  \u003cmark>-7\u003c/mark>   3   1   3\ncur   0    need -7    matches 0\nseen  {0: 2, 3: 1, 7: 1}\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 3: \u003ccode>cur = 3\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>cur - k = -4\u003c/code>: not seen, so no subarray ends here; then record 3\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  -7   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   1   3\ncur   3    need -4    matches 0\nseen  {0: 2, 3: 2, 7: 1}\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 4: \u003ccode>cur = 4\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>cur - k = -3\u003c/code>: not seen, so no subarray ends here; then record 4\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  -7   3   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   3\ncur   4    need -3    matches 0\nseen  {0: 2, 3: 2, 7: 1, 4: 1}\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i = 5: \u003ccode>cur = 7\u003c/code>, look up \u003ccode>0\u003c/code>: seen 2 times, the empty prefix and the prefix after i = 2, so \u003ccode>a[0:6]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>a[3:6] = [3, 1, 3]\u003c/code> both sum to 7\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         3   4  -7   3   1   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\ncur   7    need 0    matches 2\nseen  {0: 2, 3: 2, 7: 2, 4: 1}\ncount 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Recording before looking up:\u003c/b> counts empty subarrays whenever k = 0.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>No seed:\u003c/b> misses every subarray that starts at index 0; for the longest variant, seeding \u003ccode>{0: 0}\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>{0: -1}\u003c/code> makes those lengths one too short.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Overwriting first indices\u003c/b> in the longest variant shortens answers.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Using it where a window is better:\u003c/b> with non-negative values and a \"sum at most k\" condition, a sliding window needs O(1) space. The map is for exact targets and signed data.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>count_sum_k([0, 0, 0], 0)\u003c/code> return, and what would it return if the loop recorded \u003ccode>cur\u003c/code> before the lookup?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>6, one for each of the 3 + 2 + 1 non-empty subarrays. Recording first lets each step also match itself (an empty subarray ending there), adding 3 more: 9.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to count or measure subarrays with an exact sum in one pass by asking the map how many earlier prefixes equal \u003ccode>cur - k\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/remainders-xor-and-balance-as-prefix-keys":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Many subarray conditions depend only on a \u003cb>key\u003c/b> of the two bounding prefixes: equal remainders mod k mean the sum is divisible by k, prefix XORs that differ by k (in XOR terms) mean the subarray's XOR is k, and equal balances (with 0 scored as -1) mean equal counts. Run the same one-pass scan as the prefix map, but store the key instead of the raw sum.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Question about \u003ccode>a[j:i]\u003c/code>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Key per prefix\u003c/th>\u003cth>Look up\u003c/th>\u003cth>Map value\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sum divisible by k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>pre % k\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the same remainder\u003c/td>\u003ctd>count\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sum a multiple of k, length at least 2\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>pre % k\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the same remainder, two or more positions back\u003c/td>\u003ctd>first index\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>XOR equals k\u003c/td>\u003ctd>prefix XOR\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>cur ^ k\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>count\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>As many 0s as 1s\u003c/td>\u003ctd>sum with 0 scored as -1\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the same balance\u003c/td>\u003ctd>first index\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Every vowel an even number of times\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5-bit parity mask\u003c/td>\u003ctd>the same mask\u003c/td>\u003ctd>first index\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why remainders work:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>(pre[i] - pre[j]) % k == 0\u003c/code> exactly when \u003ccode>pre[i] % k == pre[j] % k\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>XOR is its own inverse:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>px[i] ^ px[j]\u003c/code> is the XOR of \u003ccode>a[j:i]\u003c/code>, so the XOR is k exactly when \u003ccode>px[j] == px[i] ^ k\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Negative remainders:\u003c/b> Python's \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> takes the sign of the divisor, so \u003ccode>-5 % 3 == 1\u003c/code>. C++ and Java truncate towards zero (\u003ccode>-5 % 3 == -2\u003c/code>); normalise with \u003ccode>((x % k) + k) % k\u003c/code> or equal remainders will not match.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Small key spaces:\u003c/b> with keys in \u003ccode>0..k-1\u003c/code> (or 32 parity masks), a list of size k replaces the dict: O(k) space and no hashing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Counting pairs at the end:\u003c/b> a key seen c times contributes c(c - 1)/2 subarrays; the running version adds the same total one step at a time.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import Counter\n\n\ndef count_equal_keys(a, step, start=0):\n    \"\"\"Subarrays whose two bounding prefix keys are equal; key_(i+1) = step(key_i, a[i]).\"\"\"\n    seen = Counter({start: 1})\n    key = start\n    count = 0\n    for x in a:\n        key = step(key, x)\n        count += seen[key]\n        seen[key] += 1\n    return count\n\n\ndef count_xor_k(a, k):\n    \"\"\"Subarrays whose XOR is exactly k: look up cur ^ k instead of cur.\"\"\"\n    seen = Counter({0: 1})\n    cur = count = 0\n    for x in a:\n        cur ^= x\n        count += seen[cur ^ k]     # px[j] ^ px[i] == k  &lt;=&gt;  px[j] == px[i] ^ k\n        seen[cur] += 1\n    return count\n\n\n# Sums divisible by k: count_equal_keys(a, lambda s, x: (s + x) % k)\n# XOR equal to zero:   count_equal_keys(a, lambda s, x: s ^ x)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the subarrays of \u003ccode>[2, -7, 4, 3, 5]\u003c/code> whose sum is divisible by 3.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Seed remainder 0 once (the empty prefix). \u003ccode>seen[r]\u003c/code> counts earlier prefixes with remainder r\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   4   3   5\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=0  r2=0\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix 2, remainder 2: no earlier prefix has remainder 2\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>  -7   4   3   5\nprefix    2\n% 3       \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=0  r2=1\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix -5: Python gives \u003ccode>-5 % 3 = 1\u003c/code> (C++ and Java give -2). No earlier remainder 1\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  \u003cmark>-7\u003c/mark>   4   3   5\nprefix    2  -5\n% 3       2   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=1  r2=1\ncount 0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix -1, remainder 2: 1 earlier prefix shares it, so 1 new subarray ends here\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   3   5\nprefix    2  -5  -1\n% 3       2   1   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=1  r2=2\ncount 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix 2, remainder 2: 2 earlier prefixes share it, so 2 new subarrays end here\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   4   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   5\nprefix    2  -5  -1   2\n% 3       2   1   2   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=1  r2=3\ncount 3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix 7, remainder 1: 1 earlier prefix shares it, so 1 new subarray ends here\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   4   3   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>\nprefix    2  -5  -1   2   7\n% 3       2   1   2   2   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>\nseen by remainder  r0=1  r1=2  r2=3\ncount 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Total 4: \u003ccode>[-7, 4]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[-7, 4, 3]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>[3]\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[4, 3, 5]\u003c/code>. The last one pairs prefixes -5 and 7, which C-style \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> would miss\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a         2  -7   4   3   5\nprefix    2  -5  -1   2   7\n% 3       2   1   2   2   1\ncount 4\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>C-style remainders\u003c/b> in C++ and Java break the matching for negative prefixes (see the last step above).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>\"Length at least 2\"\u003c/b> needs positions, not counts: store the first index of each remainder and require \u003ccode>i - first &gt;= 2\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Seeds:\u003c/b> remainder 0 counted once for counts; balance 0 at index -1 for longest-length questions.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>More than two symbols:\u003c/b> scoring 0 as -1 only works for two categories; for three, use a tuple of differences such as \u003ccode>(count_a - count_b, count_b - count_c)\u003c/code> as the key.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In C++, \u003ccode>-5 % 3\u003c/code> is -2. In the worked example, which subarray would a direct C++ translation miss, and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>[4, 3, 5]\u003c/code>: its bounding prefixes are -5 and 7, whose remainders are -2 and 1 in C++, so they do not match. Normalise with \u003ccode>((x % k) + k) % k\u003c/code>; Python's \u003ccode>%\u003c/code> already does.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What is the longest stretch of \u003ccode>[1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 1]\u003c/code> with equally many 0s and 1s?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>6. Scoring 0 as -1 gives balances 1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 0, 1. Balance 0 was first seen at the seed index -1 and again at index 5 (length 6), and balance 1 first at index 0 and again at index 6 (also length 6).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to rephrase divisibility, XOR and balance conditions as \"two prefixes share a key\" and count them in one pass.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/2d-prefix-sums-and-integral-images":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Let \u003ccode>P[r][c]\u003c/code> be the sum of everything above and to the left of the corner (r, c), with an extra row and column of zeros. A rectangle \u003ccode>[r1, r2) x [c1, c2)\u003c/code> is then \u003ccode>P[r2][c2] - P[r1][c2] - P[r2][c1] + P[r1][c1]\u003c/code>: take the big block, remove the strip above and the strip to the left, and add back the corner you removed twice. O(mn) to build, O(1) per query.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>           c1         c2\n     +----------+---------+\n     |    A     |    B    |     P[r1][c1] = A\n  r1 +----------+---------+     P[r1][c2] = A + B\n     |    C     |  query  |     P[r2][c1] = A + C\n  r2 +----------+---------+     P[r2][c2] = A + B + C + query\n\n  query = P[r2][c2] - P[r1][c2] - P[r2][c1] + P[r1][c1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Build with the same idea:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>P[r+1][c+1] = g[r][c] + P[r][c+1] + P[r+1][c] - P[r][c]\u003c/code>, one O(1) step per cell.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Integral images:\u003c/b> computer vision calls this table an integral image. The Viola-Jones face detector evaluates each rectangular Haar feature with a few lookups, so a feature costs the same at every size and scale; a box blur costs the same per pixel at any radius. OpenCV provides it as \u003ccode>cv2.integral\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>NumPy:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>P[1:, 1:] = g.cumsum(axis=0, dtype=np.int64).cumsum(axis=1)\u003c/code> builds it in C. Choose the dtype deliberately: an all-white 5000 x 5000 image sums to 6.4 x 10^9, which wraps a uint32 table (the lab shows it).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fix a band, collapse to 1D:\u003c/b> to count sub-rectangles with sum t, loop over row pairs (top, bottom), keep running column sums for the band, and run the 1D prefix-map count on them. With R rows and C columns that is O(R^2 C), so put the smaller dimension on the outside.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Higher dimensions:\u003c/b> the same pattern uses 2^d corners with alternating signs.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def build_2d(grid):\n    \"\"\"P[r][c] = sum of grid[:r][:c] (the rectangle above and left of (r, c)).\"\"\"\n    m = len(grid)\n    n = len(grid[0]) if m else 0\n    P = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n    for r in range(m):\n        for c in range(n):\n            P[r + 1][c + 1] = grid[r][c] + P[r][c + 1] + P[r + 1][c] - P[r][c]\n    return P\n\n\ndef rect_sum(P, r1, c1, r2, c2):\n    \"\"\"Sum of grid[r1:r2][c1:c2] (half-open in both directions), O(1).\"\"\"\n    return P[r2][c2] - P[r1][c2] - P[r2][c1] + P[r1][c1]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The grid and the query: rows 1 to 2, columns 1 to 2, half-open \u003ccode>[1, 3) x [1, 3)\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>grid\n   3   0   1   4\n   5   \u003cmark>6\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   2\n   1   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>0\u003c/mark>   1\n   4   1   0   5\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Build \u003ccode>P\u003c/code> (one extra zero row and column). For example \u003ccode>P[2][2] = g[1][1] + P[1][2] + P[2][1] - P[1][1] = 6 + 3 + 8 - 3 = 14\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>P\n   0   0   0   0   0\n   0   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   4   8\n   0   \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>  \u003cmark>14\u003c/mark>  18  24\n   0   9  17  21  28\n   0  13  22  26  38\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Query: start from \u003ccode>P[3][3] = 21\u003c/code>, everything above and left of the bottom-right corner\u003c/p>\u003cpre>P\n   0   0   0   0   0\n   0   3   3   4   8\n   0   8  14  18  24\n   0   9  17  \u003cmark>21\u003c/mark>  28\n   0  13  22  26  38\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Subtract \u003ccode>P[1][3] = 4\u003c/code> (rows above the box) and \u003ccode>P[3][1] = 9\u003c/code> (columns left of it)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>P\n   0   0   0   0   0\n   0   3   3   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   8\n   0   8  14  18  24\n   0   \u003cmark>9\u003c/mark>  17  21  28\n   0  13  22  26  38\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">The top-left block \u003ccode>P[1][1] = 3\u003c/code> was subtracted twice, so add it back: 21 - 4 - 9 + 3 = 11 = 6 + 3 + 2 + 0\u003c/p>\u003cpre>P\n   0   0   0   0   0\n   0   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   3   4   8\n   0   8  14  18  24\n   0   9  17  21  28\n   0  13  22  26  38\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inclusive corners:\u003c/b> for an inclusive rectangle (r1, c1) to (r2, c2), shift the far corner: \u003ccode>P[r2+1][c2+1] - P[r1][c2+1] - P[r2+1][c1] + P[r1][c1]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Aliased rows:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>[[0] * (n + 1)] * (m + 1)\u003c/code> makes every row the same list; build rows with a comprehension.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fixed-width overflow\u003c/b> in NumPy, C++ and Java tables.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Empty grids:\u003c/b> \u003ccode>len(grid[0])\u003c/code> raises on \u003ccode>[]\u003c/code>; guard it as the template does.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Using the table \u003ccode>P\u003c/code> in the trace, what is the sum of the bottom row of the grid?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It is the rectangle \u003ccode>[3, 4) x [0, 4)\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>P[4][4] - P[3][4] - P[4][0] + P[3][0] = 38 - 28 - 0 + 0 = 10\u003c/code>, which matches 4 + 1 + 0 + 5.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to read any rectangle sum from four corners of an integral image, and how to collapse a 2D counting problem to 1D by fixing a band of rows.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/difference-arrays-many-range-updates-one-rebuild":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The difference array \u003ccode>d[i] = a[i] - a[i - 1]\u003c/code> is the inverse of the prefix sum: a running sum of \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> gives \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> back. Adding v to the whole slice \u003ccode>a[l:r]\u003c/code> changes only two differences, \u003ccode>d[l] += v\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[r] -= v\u003c/code>. So record each update in O(1) and rebuild once at the end in O(n): O(n + q) instead of O(nq).\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inverse operations:\u003c/b> prefix sums and differences undo each other, like integrals and derivatives. A range addition is a step up at l and a step down at r.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Spare slot:\u003c/b> keep \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> one longer than \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> so an update ending at \u003ccode>r == n\u003c/code> needs no bounds check.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Starting from a non-zero array:\u003c/b> either start \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> from the differences of \u003ccode>a\u003c/code>, or rebuild the additions from zeros and add them to \u003ccode>a\u003c/code> element by element.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Sweep-line view:\u003c/b> +v at each start and -v at each end, processed in position order, tracks \"how many are active right now\" (passengers in a car, meetings in progress, flips covering this bit). You can consume \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> while still writing it, left to right, as long as every new update starts at or after the current position.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>2D:\u003c/b> for \u003ccode>[r1, r2) x [c1, c2)\u003c/code> add +v at (r1, c1), -v at (r1, c2), -v at (r2, c1) and +v at (r2, c2), then take running sums along rows and then down columns.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Huge coordinates:\u003c/b> if positions reach 10^9, sort the start and end events (or compress the coordinates) instead of allocating an array.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The limit:\u003c/b> a read between updates needs a rebuild each time. Interleaved range updates and point reads call for a Fenwick tree over \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> (the range-query module).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from itertools import accumulate\n\n\ndef apply_range_adds(n, updates):\n    \"\"\"Each (l, r, v) adds v to a[l:r] (half-open); returns the final array, O(n + len(updates)).\"\"\"\n    d = [0] * (n + 1)              # one spare slot so r == n needs no check\n    for l, r, v in updates:\n        d[l] += v\n        d[r] -= v\n    return list(accumulate(d[:n]))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Three updates on six zeros: +2 on \u003ccode>a[1:4]\u003c/code>, +3 on \u003ccode>a[2:6]\u003c/code> and -1 on \u003ccode>a[0:2]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Start with \u003ccode>d = [0] * (n + 1)\u003c/code>; slot 6 is the spare that lets \u003ccode>r == n\u003c/code> work without a check\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd         0   0   0   0   0   0   0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add +2 to \u003ccode>a[1:4]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>d[1] += 2\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[4] -= 2\u003c/code>. Two writes, however long the range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd         0   \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>   0   0  \u003cmark>-2\u003c/mark>   0   0\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add +3 to \u003ccode>a[2:6]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>d[2] += 3\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[6] -= 3\u003c/code>. Two writes, however long the range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd         0   2   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   0  -2   0  \u003cmark>-3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Add -1 to \u003ccode>a[0:2]\u003c/code>: \u003ccode>d[0] -= 1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[2] += 1\u003c/code>. Two writes, however long the range\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd        \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>   2   \u003cmark>4\u003c/mark>   0  -2   0  -3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Rebuild with one running sum over \u003ccode>d[:6]\u003c/code>: each value is the total of every start minus every stop so far\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5   6\nd        -1   2   4   0  -2   0  -3\na        \u003cmark>-1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>1\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>   \u003cmark>3\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Check one cell: \u003ccode>a[2]\u003c/code> is inside \u003ccode>[1, 4)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>[2, 6)\u003c/code> but not \u003ccode>[0, 2)\u003c/code>, so 2 + 3 = 5\u003c/p>\u003cpre>i         0   1   2   3   4   5\na        -1   1   \u003cmark>5\u003c/mark>   5   3   3\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inclusive, 1-indexed inputs:\u003c/b> for \u003ccode>[first, last]\u003c/code>, write \u003ccode>d[first - 1] += v\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>d[last] -= v\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Reading \u003ccode>d[i]\u003c/code> as a value\u003c/b> before the rebuild.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The 2D sign pattern:\u003c/b> two minus corners, two plus corners; a wrong sign leaks the update across the grid.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Allocating 10^9 slots\u003c/b> when only a few thousand positions matter.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Five counters start at 0. Add 5 to positions 1 to 3, 10 to positions 2 to 4 and 7 to positions 4 to 5 (1-indexed, inclusive). What is \u003ccode>d\u003c/code> before the rebuild, and what are the final values?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The 0-indexed half-open updates are \u003ccode>(0, 3, 5)\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>(1, 4, 10)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>(3, 5, 7)\u003c/code>, so \u003ccode>d = [5, 10, 0, 2, -10, -7]\u003c/code> and the running sum gives \u003ccode>[5, 15, 15, 17, 7]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to batch any number of range additions into two writes each and recover every value with one running sum.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","l:prefix/left-and-right-passes":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>When the answer at index i combines everything to its left with everything to its right, compute \u003ccode>left[i]\u003c/code> (an aggregate of \u003ccode>a[:i]\u003c/code>) in a forward pass and \u003ccode>right[i]\u003c/code> (of \u003ccode>a[i+1:]\u003c/code>) in a backward pass, then combine them per index. Any associative operation with an identity works: sum, product, max, min, gcd. A close cousin keeps a running extreme of the prefix sums themselves.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Internals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Exclusive scans\u003c/b> generalise prefix sums to any associative operation; \"exclusive\" means \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> itself is left out of both sides.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Why not subtract from the total:\u003c/b> subtraction works for sums (the right side of i is \u003ccode>total - left - a[i]\u003c/code>) but there is no inverse for max or min, and dividing a product by \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> fails when \u003ccode>a[i] == 0\u003c/code>. Two passes need no inverse.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>O(1) extra space:\u003c/b> write \u003ccode>left\u003c/code> straight into the output array, then sweep backwards multiplying by a running right aggregate.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Running extremes of prefixes:\u003c/b> the best subarray sum ending at i is \u003ccode>pre[i + 1] - min(pre[0..i])\u003c/code>; the largest absolute subarray sum is \u003ccode>max(pre) - min(pre)\u003c/code> over all prefixes (the empty one included), because the absolute value makes their order irrelevant.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Best on each side:\u003c/b> to choose non-overlapping windows, precompute \u003ccode>best_left[i]\u003c/code> (the best window ending at or before i) and \u003ccode>best_right[i]\u003c/code> (the best starting at or after i), then try every middle choice and combine in O(1). When the earliest indices must win ties, use \u003ccode>&gt;\u003c/code> in the forward pass and \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code> in the backward one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Elsewhere in the route:\u003c/b> trapping rain water uses left and right maxima (two pointers module), and next-greater problems refine \"everything to my right\" with a monotonic stack.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Template\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def exclusive_scans(a, op, identity):\n    \"\"\"left[i] = op over a[:i] and right[i] = op over a[i+1:], both O(n).\"\"\"\n    n = len(a)\n    left, right = [identity] * n, [identity] * n\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        left[i] = op(left[i - 1], a[i - 1])\n    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n        right[i] = op(right[i + 1], a[i + 1])\n    return left, right\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Left and right maxima of \u003ccode>[2, 7, 1, 8, 2, 8]\u003c/code>, using \u003ccode>max\u003c/code> with identity \u003ccode>-inf\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Left pass: \u003ccode>left[i] = max(left[i - 1], a[i - 1])\u003c/code>, the largest value strictly before i (identity -inf at i = 0)\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          2    7    1    8    2    8\nleft    \u003cmark>-inf\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Fill left to right; each entry costs one comparison\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          2    7    1    8    2    8\nleft    -inf    \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Right pass from the end: \u003ccode>right[i] = max(right[i + 1], a[i + 1])\u003c/code>, the largest value strictly after i\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          2    7    1    8    2    8\nleft    -inf    2    7    7    8    8\nright      \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark> \u003cmark>-inf\u003c/mark>\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">Combine per index in O(1): \u003ccode>a[i] &gt; left[i]\u003c/code> marks a new record, at indices [0, 1, 3]; swap max for multiplication and \u003ccode>left[i] * right[i]\u003c/code> is the product of everything except \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code>\u003c/p>\u003cpre>a          \u003cmark>2\u003c/mark>    \u003cmark>7\u003c/mark>    1    \u003cmark>8\u003c/mark>    2    8\nleft    -inf    2    7    7    8    8\nright      8    8    8    8    8 -inf\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Wrong identity:\u003c/b> 0 is not the identity for max over negative numbers; use \u003ccode>float(\"-inf\")\u003c/code> (or 1 for products, 0 for sums and gcd).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Inclusive versus exclusive\u003c/b> scans differ by one position; decide whether \u003ccode>a[i]\u003c/code> belongs on a side.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Ties\u003c/b> in best-left and best-right arrays when the smallest indices must win.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Division shortcuts\u003c/b> that break on zeros.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Quick check\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Run the two passes with multiplication on \u003ccode>[3, 1, 2, 5]\u003c/code>. What are \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> and the product of everything except each element?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003ccode>left = [1, 3, 3, 6]\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>right = [10, 10, 5, 1]\u003c/code>, and the products are \u003ccode>[10, 30, 15, 6]\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"co co-know\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">You now know\u003c/p>\u003cp>You now know how to answer \"everything except me\" and \"best on each side\" questions with one pass from each end and an O(1) combine per index.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>","lab:prefix/integral-image-region-sums-and-a-box-blur-at-any":"\u003cp>You will compute region sums over a 2000 x 2000 image naively and with an integral image, check that both agree on 10,000 random rectangles, apply a box blur at four radii both ways, and watch a too-narrow integer type wrap.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.10+ with \u003ccode>pip install numpy\u003c/code>; about 1 GB of free memory.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save this as \u003ccode>integral_lab.py\u003c/code> and run it with \u003ccode>python integral_lab.py\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\nimport numpy as np\n\nH, W = 2000, 2000\nrng = np.random.default_rng(1)\nimg = rng.integers(0, 256, size=(H, W), dtype=np.uint8)\n\n\ndef integral(img):\n    \"\"\"P[r, c] = img[:r, :c].sum(); one extra row and column of zeros.\"\"\"\n    P = np.zeros((img.shape[0] + 1, img.shape[1] + 1), dtype=np.int64)\n    P[1:, 1:] = img.cumsum(axis=0, dtype=np.int64).cumsum(axis=1)\n    return P\n\n\ndef region_sum(P, r1, c1, r2, c2):\n    \"\"\"img[r1:r2, c1:c2].sum() in four lookups.\"\"\"\n    return int(P[r2, c2] - P[r1, c2] - P[r2, c1] + P[r1, c1])\n\n\nt = time.perf_counter()\nP = integral(img)\nprint(f\"build integral image: {(time.perf_counter() - t) * 1000:.1f} ms\")\n\nrects = []\nfor _ in range(10_000):\n    r1, r2 = sorted(rng.integers(0, H + 1, size=2))\n    c1, c2 = sorted(rng.integers(0, W + 1, size=2))\n    rects.append((int(r1), int(c1), int(r2), int(c2)))\n\nt = time.perf_counter()\nnaive = [int(img[r1:r2, c1:c2].sum(dtype=np.int64)) for r1, c1, r2, c2 in rects]\nnaive_s = time.perf_counter() - t\nt = time.perf_counter()\nfast = [region_sum(P, *q) for q in rects]\nfast_s = time.perf_counter() - t\nassert naive == fast\nprint(f\"10,000 rectangles: naive {naive_s:.2f} s, integral {fast_s * 1000:.1f} ms, \"\n      f\"{naive_s / fast_s:.0f}x faster, all equal\")\n\n\ndef box_blur_shifts(img, r):\n    \"\"\"Mean over a (2r+1) x (2r+1) window by adding shifted copies: (2r+1)^2 whole-image adds.\"\"\"\n    pad = np.pad(img.astype(np.int64), r, mode=\"edge\")\n    acc = np.zeros(img.shape, dtype=np.int64)\n    for dy in range(2 * r + 1):\n        for dx in range(2 * r + 1):\n            acc += pad[dy:dy + img.shape[0], dx:dx + img.shape[1]]\n    return acc // (2 * r + 1) ** 2\n\n\ndef box_blur_integral(img, r):\n    \"\"\"The same blur with four whole-array lookups into the integral image of the padded image.\"\"\"\n    pad = np.pad(img, r, mode=\"edge\")\n    P = integral(pad)\n    k = 2 * r + 1\n    h, w = img.shape\n    s = P[k:k + h, k:k + w] - P[0:h, k:k + w] - P[k:k + h, 0:w] + P[0:h, 0:w]\n    return s // (k * k)\n\n\nfor r in (1, 3, 7, 15):\n    t = time.perf_counter(); a = box_blur_shifts(img, r); ts = time.perf_counter() - t\n    t = time.perf_counter(); b = box_blur_integral(img, r); ti = time.perf_counter() - t\n    assert np.array_equal(a, b)\n    print(f\"box blur r={r:&gt;2}: shifted adds {ts * 1000:7.0f} ms, integral image {ti * 1000:5.0f} ms\")\n\nwhite = np.full((5000, 5000), 255, dtype=np.uint8)\nP32 = np.zeros((5001, 5001), dtype=np.uint32)\nP32[1:, 1:] = white.cumsum(axis=0, dtype=np.uint32).cumsum(axis=1, dtype=np.uint32)\nprint(\"uint32 corner:\", int(P32[-1, -1]), \" true total:\", 255 * 5000 * 5000)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Before you read the output, predict: how many lookups does one region sum take, and how many whole-image additions does the shifted blur do at r = 15?\u003c/li>\u003cli>Draw the four corners used by \u003ccode>box_blur_integral\u003c/code> for one pixel on paper and check them against \u003ccode>rect_sum\u003c/code> from the 2D recap: the slices are that formula applied to every pixel at once.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change \u003ccode>rects\u003c/code> to only tiny rectangles (side at most 5). Does the naive approach catch up? Explain why from what each method touches.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Fix the overflow line by choosing a dtype that cannot wrap, and state the largest image of 8-bit pixels that a uint32 table can hold safely (255 x H x W must stay below 2^32).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cp>On a recent laptop or server, single-threaded:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>Building the integral image takes roughly 100 to 250 ms; it is two passes of C loops over 4 million pixels.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The naive region sums take several seconds for 10,000 random rectangles (the average rectangle covers about a ninth of the image, roughly 440,000 pixels), while the integral image answers all of them in a few tens of milliseconds: a few hundred times faster, and every answer equal.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The shifted-copy blur grows with (2r + 1)^2: roughly 0.1 to 0.2 s at r = 1, 2 to 2.5 s at r = 7 and around 10 s at r = 15. The integral blur stays near 0.1 to 0.25 s at every radius, because it always does four lookups per pixel. At r = 1 the two are about level, or the shifted version wins: nine whole-image additions are cheap, and the integral version also pays to build its table.\u003c/li>\u003cli>With tiny rectangles the naive sums drop to tens of milliseconds for all 10,000 and the gap shrinks to a few times: each call's Python and NumPy overhead now costs more than the arithmetic. The table's advantage is that its cost never depends on the area.\u003c/li>\u003cli>The uint32 corner prints 2080032704 instead of 6375000000: the true total wrapped modulo 2^32 without any error. A uint32 table is only safe while 255 x H x W &lt; 2^32, about 16.8 million pixels (for example 4096 x 4096).\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>","p:303":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Prepare an unchanged integer array so that repeated requests can return the sum from a given left index through a given right index, including both endpoints. Construct \u003ccode>NumArray\u003c/code> once and call \u003ccode>sumRange\u003c/code> for each request.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Indices are zero based and valid, with left no greater than right. The array can contain negative values, and many queries can share the same array. There are no updates after construction, so preprocessing can be reused. A single query may cover the whole array; summing its elements every time ignores this reuse.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Many sum queries over different ranges of an array that never changes after construction.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Doing the work per query costs O(n) each time. What could you compute once so that each query becomes a couple of lookups?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sum array\u003c/p>\u003cp>Store pre with pre[0] = 0 and pre[i + 1] = pre[i] + nums[i]; the inclusive range left..right is pre[right + 1] - pre[left]. Building is O(n) and every query is two lookups and one subtraction.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) build, O(1) per query, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each query, visit each element in the requested interval and add it. This takes time proportional to the interval length, up to O(n) per query, and constant extra space. It is a useful independent baseline, particularly for singleton and full-array intervals.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let prefix position \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> contain the sum of the first \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> elements. The sum through \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> contains the sum before \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>, so subtracting the latter removes exactly the unwanted portion. The initial zero represents an empty prefix and makes the same formula work when the range begins at index zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [3, -2, 5]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">prefix = [0, 3, 1, 6]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">query [1, 2]: prefix[3] - prefix[1] = 6 - 3 = 3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">query [0, 0]: prefix[1] - prefix[0] = 3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class NumArray:\n    def __init__(self, nums):\n        self.prefix = [0]\n        for value in nums:\n            self.prefix.append(self.prefix[-1] + value)\n\n    def sumRange(self, left, right):\n        return self.prefix[right + 1] - self.prefix[left]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Construction takes O(n) time and O(n) space. Each query takes O(1) time and no additional space. The storage belongs to the object and is reused across calls.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Do not subtract the prefix ending at \u003ccode>left\u003c/code>: that would also remove the left endpoint. Do not use \u003ccode>right\u003c/code> instead of \u003ccode>right + 1\u003c/code>. Negative numbers do not invalidate subtraction, and a one-element query uses exactly the same formula. If the array changes later, the stored sums become stale.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For point updates between queries, consider a Fenwick tree instead of a static prefix array.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a half-open query \u003ccode>[left, right)\u003c/code>, use \u003ccode>prefix[right] - prefix[left]\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:724":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Find the earliest index in an integer array whose elements strictly before it sum to the same value as those strictly after it. Return -1 if no such index exists. An empty side has sum zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The array may contain positive, zero, and negative integers. Endpoints are legitimate candidates, and several positions may satisfy the condition. Returning the first rules out merely finding any balanced index. Since the full sum is available in one pass, the right side need not be recomputed at every position.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Find an index where the total on its left equals the total on its right.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>If you know the total of the whole array and the sum to the left of i, what is the sum to the right of i?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix and suffix passes\u003c/p>\u003cp>Keep a running left sum; index i balances when left == total - left - nums[i]. Test before adding nums[i] to left and return the first index that balances, or -1. One pass after computing the total.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Try each index and separately sum the portions on both sides. Each pair of sums costs O(n), producing O(n²) time and O(1) auxiliary space if the ranges are traversed without copying. This directly reflects the definition and is convenient for testing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Before inspecting a candidate, maintain the sum strictly to its left. The whole-array total equals left plus the current element plus right, so the right sum is \u003ccode>total - left - current\u003c/code>. Check equality before adding the current value to left. The invariant is true initially because the first index has an empty left side; adding the current value establishes it for the next index.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [1, 7, 3, 6, 5, 6], total = 28\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=0: left=0, right=27; advance left to 1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=1: left=1, right=20; advance left to 8\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=2: left=8, right=17; advance left to 11\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=3: left=11, right=11; return 3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Solution:\n    def pivotIndex(self, nums):\n        total = sum(nums)\n        left = 0\n        for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n            if left == total - left - value:\n                return i\n            left += value\n        return -1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Computing the total and scanning the candidates take O(n) time overall. The running totals and index use O(1) auxiliary space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Adding the candidate before testing includes it on the wrong side. Both sides of a single-element array are empty, so index zero qualifies. Negative values can produce surprising ties, and all-zero arrays have multiple answers; scanning in order and returning immediately chooses the earliest one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return every balanced index by collecting matches instead of returning early.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Balance weighted values by replacing each element with its weighted contribution before applying the same invariant.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:560":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the nonempty contiguous slices of an integer array whose elements add to a given target \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. Overlapping slices count separately, as do different slices with the same contents.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Entries and the target may be negative or zero. Extending a slice can therefore either raise or lower its sum, so the usual monotone sliding-window argument does not apply. Repeated prefix sums are meaningful: they represent distinct starting positions, not duplicate data to discard. The result is a count rather than the length of one slice.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count contiguous subarrays with an exact sum, and the values may be negative.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A window cannot decide when to shrink once values can be negative. Write the sum of nums[j:i] as a difference of two prefix sums: which earlier value are you looking for at step i?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix sums with a hash map\u003c/p>\u003cp>nums[j:i] sums to k exactly when pre[j] = pre[i] - k, so keep a Counter of earlier prefixes seeded with {0: 1}; at each step add seen[cur - k], then record cur. Looking up before recording keeps every counted subarray non-empty.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose every left endpoint, extend the right endpoint while maintaining a running sum, and increment the answer whenever that sum equals \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. There are O(n²) slices and each extension costs O(1), so the time is O(n²) and the extra space is O(1). This works with negative values and gives a straightforward oracle.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Define \u003ccode>P[t]\u003c/code> as the sum of the first \u003ccode>t\u003c/code> elements, including \u003ccode>P[0] = 0\u003c/code>. Slice \u003ccode>[s, t)\u003c/code> has sum \u003ccode>P[t] - P[s]\u003c/code>; it reaches the target exactly when \u003ccode>P[s] = P[t] - k\u003c/code>. As each endpoint \u003ccode>t\u003c/code> arrives, count how many earlier prefixes have that value. A frequency map preserves all matching starts. Then record the current prefix for later endpoints.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The map invariant is that it contains precisely prefixes with positions strictly before the endpoint currently being counted. Seeding the empty prefix permits slices starting at zero. Looking up before inserting also prevents counting an empty slice when \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> is zero. Every nonempty slice is associated with its unique ending position and is counted once there.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [1, -1, 1], k = 1; seen = {0: 1}, sum = 0, answer = 0\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">value 1: sum=1, seen[0]=1, answer=1; record 1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">value -1: sum=0, seen[-1]=0, answer=1; record 0\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">value 1: sum=1, seen[0]=2, answer=3; record 1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict\n\nclass Solution:\n    def subarraySum(self, nums, k):\n        seen = defaultdict(int)\n        seen[0] = 1\n        total = answer = 0\n        for value in nums:\n            total += value\n            answer += seen[total - k]\n            seen[total] += 1\n        return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The scan takes expected O(n) time with hash-map operations and O(n) space for up to n distinct prefix values. Python integers avoid fixed-width overflow; in a fixed-width language the sum and answer may need a wider type.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A set loses multiplicity, for example when zeros create several equal prefixes. Inserting before querying adds a spurious empty slice for target zero. The initial zero prefix must have frequency one, not zero. Neither a negative running sum nor a target of zero requires a special case.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Store the earliest index for each prefix instead of frequencies to find the longest target-sum slice.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Store the latest index to find a shortest nonempty target-sum slice, taking care to query first.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:974":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count nonempty contiguous slices whose sum is divisible by a positive integer \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. Elements may be negative, and different positions define different slices even if their values are identical.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The divisor is positive. Array entries may have either sign, so no increasing-window invariant is available. Divisibility depends only on one of \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> remainder classes, not on the full value of each prefix. A dense table is appropriate when O(k) storage is acceptable; for a very large divisor a sparse map can hold only remainders that occur.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count subarrays whose sum is a multiple of k, with negative values allowed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When is pre[i] - pre[j] a multiple of k? Compare the two prefixes modulo k.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix remainders, XOR and balance\u003c/p>\u003cp>A difference is divisible by k exactly when both prefixes leave the same remainder, so count remainders in a list of size k (remainder 0 seeded once) and add the count of the current remainder before incrementing it. Python's % already returns 0..k-1 for negative sums; in C++ or Java normalise with ((x % k) + k) % k.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + k) time, O(k) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate the starting index and extend each slice with a running total, checking whether the total has remainder zero. There are O(n²) slices, giving O(n²) time and O(1) extra space. This baseline does not rely on the prefix-remainder observation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>With prefix sums \u003ccode>P\u003c/code>, a slice \u003ccode>[s, t)\u003c/code> has sum \u003ccode>P[t] - P[s]\u003c/code>. This difference is divisible by \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> exactly when the two prefixes have equal remainders modulo \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. Maintain the number of earlier prefixes in each class. For each new prefix, add the current class frequency to the answer, then increase that frequency.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Initially the zero remainder occurs once for the empty prefix. Before counting an endpoint, the frequencies describe precisely the earlier prefix positions, so each matching starting position forms a nonempty slice and every valid slice appears at its unique endpoint. Python's modulo by positive \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> yields a nonnegative remainder even for negative sums.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [4, -1, 2], k = 3; counts = [1, 0, 0], remainder=0, answer=0\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">4: remainder=1, add counts[1]=0; counts becomes [1, 1, 0]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">-1: remainder=0, add counts[0]=1; counts becomes [2, 1, 0]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">2: remainder=2, add counts[2]=0; counts becomes [2, 1, 1]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">return 1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Solution:\n    def subarraysDivByK(self, nums, k):\n        counts = [0] * k\n        counts[0] = 1\n        remainder = answer = 0\n        for value in nums:\n            remainder = (remainder + value) % k\n            answer += counts[remainder]\n            counts[remainder] += 1\n        return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Initializing the table costs O(k), and the scan costs O(n), for O(n + k) time and O(k) auxiliary space. A hash map trades the dense initialization for expected O(n) time and at most O(min(n, k)) stored classes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Record a class after counting, or an empty slice is added at each position. The empty prefix must be counted before the scan so a valid slice beginning at zero is included. When \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> is one, every nonempty slice qualifies. Languages whose remainder can be negative require normalization before indexing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count slices with a specified remainder \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> by looking for earlier remainder \u003ccode>(current - r) % k\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Determine whether any qualifying slice has length at least two by storing the earliest index for each remainder.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:525":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a binary array, return the maximum length of a contiguous slice containing the same number of zeroes and ones. Return zero if no nonempty balanced slice exists.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every entry is either zero or one. A balanced slice can begin anywhere, including index zero, and it can overlap other balanced slices. Because the goal is maximum length rather than count, retaining just a frequency for each state is insufficient: we need the earliest position at which that state occurred.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The longest stretch of a binary array with as many 0s as 1s.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Can you rewrite \"equal counts\" as \"a sum equals something\" by changing how you score each element?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix remainders, XOR and balance\u003c/p>\u003cp>Score 1 as +1 and 0 as -1; a stretch is balanced when its sum is 0, that is when two prefix balances are equal. Store the first index of each balance (seed balance 0 at index -1) and maximise i - first[balance]; never overwrite a first index.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For every left endpoint, extend the right endpoint and maintain separate counts of zeroes and ones. Whenever they agree, compare the length with the best so far. The O(n²) candidate slices can be checked in O(1) per extension, using O(1) extra space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Treat each one as +1 and each zero as -1. The sum of a slice is zero exactly when its two counts match. A zero-sum slice has equal balances immediately before and after it. At each position, therefore, find the earliest earlier position with the same balance. This gives the longest balanced slice ending here.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Seed balance zero at index -1 to represent the empty prefix before the array. Only record a balance the first time it occurs. The invariant is that the table contains the earliest index for each previously encountered balance; never overwriting it ensures no longer candidate ending at the current position is missed. Taking the maximum over all endpoints gives the global optimum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [0, 1, 0, 0, 1, 1]; first = {0: -1}, balance=0\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=0: balance=-1; record first[-1]=0\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=1: balance=0; length=1-(-1)=2\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=2: balance=-1; length=2-0=2\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=3: balance=-2; record first[-2]=3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=4: balance=-1; length=4-0=4\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=5: balance=0; length=5-(-1)=6; return 6\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Solution:\n    def findMaxLength(self, nums):\n        first = {0: -1}\n        balance = best = 0\n        for i, value in enumerate(nums):\n            balance += 1 if value == 1 else -1\n            if balance in first:\n                best = max(best, i - first[balance])\n            else:\n                first[balance] = i\n        return best\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The scan uses expected O(n) time with dictionary lookups and O(n) auxiliary space for distinct balances. There is one entry at most per position plus the initial empty prefix.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Overwriting a first occurrence with a later one shortens subsequent candidates. Initializing balance zero at index zero rather than -1 loses one element for slices beginning at the array's start. All-equal inputs correctly produce zero, while a two-element array \u003ccode>[0, 1]\u003c/code> produces two. The scoring assumes binary inputs.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The answer is always even: every qualifying slice contains two equally sized groups. That observation can help check a result, although it does not identify the optimal boundaries by itself.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Count balanced slices by storing frequencies rather than earliest indices.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a longest slice with a fixed excess of ones, look up the balance minus that excess.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:304":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Preprocess a fixed integer matrix and answer repeated requests for the sum within an inclusive, axis-aligned rectangle. A request gives top row, left column, bottom row, and right column in zero-based coordinates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The matrix is rectangular and nonempty; queried corners are valid and ordered. Values may be negative. The matrix does not change between requests, so a table of cumulative sums can be built once and reused. Rectangles can touch any boundary, making a zero-padded row and column useful.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Many rectangle-sum queries on a matrix that does not change.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Extend the 1D idea: what single number per corner would let you peel any rectangle out of a few stored values?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> 2D prefix sums\u003c/p>\u003cp>Let P[r][c] be the sum of everything above and to the left of corner (r, c) in an (m + 1) x (n + 1) table, built with P[r+1][c+1] = cell + P[r][c+1] + P[r+1][c] - P[r][c]. An inclusive rectangle (r1, c1)..(r2, c2) is P[r2+1][c2+1] - P[r1][c2+1] - P[r2+1][c1] + P[r1][c1].\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(mn) build, O(1) per query, O(mn) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each query visit every cell inside its two row and column bounds. This costs O(area) time per query, up to O(mn), and O(1) auxiliary space. With many large rectangles, the same cells are repeatedly added.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let \u003ccode>P[r][c]\u003c/code> sum the cells in rows before \u003ccode>r\u003c/code> and columns before \u003ccode>c\u003c/code>. The extra top row and left column consist of zeroes. Build a prefix from the cell itself, the prefix above, and the prefix to the left, subtracting their overlap once.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To answer a rectangle, take the prefix through its bottom-right corner, remove the strip above and the strip to the left, then restore their overlap, which was removed twice. This inclusion-exclusion leaves every inside cell exactly once and every outside cell zero times. Shifting the inclusive bottom and right coordinates by one converts them into prefix boundaries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">matrix = [[1, 2], [3, 4]]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">padded prefix rows: [0, 0, 0], [0, 1, 3], [0, 4, 10]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">query rows 0..1, column 1..1:\u003c/p>\u003cpre>P[2][2] - P[0][2] - P[2][1] + P[0][1]\n= 10 - 0 - 4 + 0 = 6\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class NumMatrix:\n    def __init__(self, matrix):\n        m, n = len(matrix), len(matrix[0])\n        self.prefix = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n        for r in range(m):\n            for c in range(n):\n                self.prefix[r + 1][c + 1] = (\n                    matrix[r][c] + self.prefix[r][c + 1]\n                    + self.prefix[r + 1][c] - self.prefix[r][c]\n                )\n\n    def sumRegion(self, row1, col1, row2, col2):\n        p = self.prefix\n        return (p[row2 + 1][col2 + 1] - p[row1][col2 + 1]\n                - p[row2 + 1][col1] + p[row1][col1])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Building the table costs O(mn) time and O(mn) space. A query uses four entries, O(1) time, and O(1) additional space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The overlap needs to be subtracted during construction and added back during a query. Independent rows are essential: multiplying a single row list aliases all rows in Python. A single cell and a rectangle covering the whole matrix are useful tests. Subsequent mutations of the input are not reflected in the prefix table.\u003c/p>\u003cp>As a concrete boundary check, a top-left anchored rectangle subtracts two zero prefixes and adds a zero overlap. For an interior rectangle, both removed strips can contain the same upper-left cells, which explains why the final addition is indispensable even when all matrix entries happen to be positive. With negative entries the same algebra applies; no monotonicity assumption is involved.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>For mutable cells, use a two-dimensional Fenwick tree or another dynamic range-sum structure.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For half-open rectangles, use the same inclusion-exclusion directly on their four prefix boundaries.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1109":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> flights numbered from one. Each booking \u003ccode>[first, last, seats]\u003c/code> adds its seat count to every flight from \u003ccode>first\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>last\u003c/code>, inclusive. Return the final seat total for every flight in order after all bookings.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Each booking has valid ordered flight numbers within \u003ccode>1..n\u003c/code>, and its seat count is positive. Flights and bookings can both be numerous, while only the final totals are requested. Processing each full interval separately can repeat the same work many times. Additions commute, so their boundary effects can be accumulated before any totals are reconstructed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Many bookings that each add seats to every flight from first to last, and you only need the final totals.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Touching every flight of every booking is O(n) per booking. Record only where each booking starts and where it stops.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Difference array\u003c/p>\u003cp>Difference array: d[first - 1] += seats and d[last] -= seats (the input is 1-indexed and inclusive), then one running sum over d gives every flight's total. The final pass costs O(n) however long the bookings are.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n + b) time for b bookings, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start with \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> zero totals. For every booking, add its seats to each flight it covers. This takes O(n + sum of booking lengths), at most O(n times b) for \u003ccode>b\u003c/code> bookings, with O(n) space for the result. It is an easy correctness oracle because it directly performs the specified updates.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Instead of storing the totals immediately, store changes between adjacent flights. An inclusive range addition starts contributing at its first flight and stops just after its last. In zero-based coordinates, add \u003ccode>seats\u003c/code> at \u003ccode>first - 1\u003c/code> and subtract it at \u003ccode>last\u003c/code>. A sentinel at index \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> safely receives a stop after the final flight.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Once all boundaries have been marked, scan left to right with a running sum. At each flight, every booking whose start has been passed contributes, and every booking whose stop has been passed no longer contributes. Thus the running value equals exactly the sum of the active bookings for that flight. Superposition makes overlapping bookings work without special cases.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n=4, bookings=[[1, 2, 5], [2, 4, 3]]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">boundary marks: [5, 3, -5, 0, -3]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">running totals at flights 1..4: 5, 8, 3, 3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">the final -3 is a sentinel outside the output\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Solution:\n    def corpFlightBookings(self, bookings, n):\n        diff = [0] * (n + 1)\n        for first, last, seats in bookings:\n            diff[first - 1] += seats\n            diff[last] -= seats\n        total = 0\n        result = []\n        for i in range(n):\n            total += diff[i]\n            result.append(total)\n        return result\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Marking \u003ccode>b\u003c/code> bookings and scanning \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> flights take O(b + n) time. The difference array and returned result each use O(n) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The stop is at \u003ccode>last\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>last - 1\u003c/code>, because the input's last flight is inclusive and one based. A booking covering the final flight writes its stop into the sentinel rather than outside the array. Multiple starts or stops at the same boundary must be added, not assigned. An empty booking list yields all zeroes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Allow signed updates to model cancellations as long as that is permitted by the surrounding application.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Apply the difference updates to an existing base array by adding each reconstructed change to its base value.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1749":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Return the largest absolute sum obtainable from a contiguous slice of an integer array. An empty slice is allowed and contributes zero, so the answer is never negative.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Values may be positive, negative, or zero. The maximum absolute sum may arise from either a large positive sum or a large negative sum, so tracking only the largest ordinary subarray is not enough. Prefix sums can rise and fall, but their largest difference in magnitude is determined by their extrema.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The largest absolute value of any subarray sum.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Every subarray sum is a difference of two prefix sums. Which pair of prefixes gives the largest absolute difference?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix and suffix passes\u003c/p>\u003cp>|pre[i] - pre[j]| is largest for the largest and the smallest prefix, the empty prefix 0 included, and with an absolute value their order does not matter. So the answer is max(pre) - min(pre), tracked in one pass with two running extremes.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(1) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate each left endpoint and accumulate the sum as the right endpoint advances. Compare the absolute value with the best answer, initialized to zero for the empty slice. This considers O(n²) nonempty slices in O(n²) time and O(1) extra space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Let \u003ccode>P[0] = 0\u003c/code>, and let \u003ccode>P[i]\u003c/code> be the sum of the first \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> elements. The sum of a slice is a difference of two prefixes. The largest possible absolute difference among any prefix values is \u003ccode>max(P) - min(P)\u003c/code>: no pair can exceed that range, and a pair attaining the two extremes achieves it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Whichever extreme appears first supplies the start boundary, so their time order does not invalidate the slice. If both extremes occur at the same position, the range is zero, which the empty slice achieves. Include the initial prefix, then update the running sum and both extrema while scanning. This is equivalent to considering both positive and negative extremes without storing the whole prefix array.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums = [2, -5, 1]; initial prefix=0, minimum=0, maximum=0\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">after 2: prefix=2, minimum=0, maximum=2\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">after -5: prefix=-3, minimum=-3, maximum=2\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">after 1: prefix=-2, minimum=-3, maximum=2\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">return 2 - (-3) = 5, achieved by the slice [-5]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Solution:\n    def maxAbsoluteSum(self, nums):\n        total = low = high = 0\n        for value in nums:\n            total += value\n            low = min(low, total)\n            high = max(high, total)\n        return high - low\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One scan takes O(n) time, and the running prefix and extrema take O(1) auxiliary space. No slice or prefix table is materialized.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Omitting the initial zero prefix misses an optimal slice beginning at the first element. The minimum prefix may appear after the maximum: absolute value permits either ordering. All-zero input returns zero. In fixed-width arithmetic, account for the range of cumulative sums and their difference.\u003c/p>\u003cp>An array of entirely negative entries can have its optimum across the whole array; a maximum-positive-sum-only method would miss it. Similarly, returning the larger absolute prefix alone misses a slice whose two endpoints are both away from the beginning.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Track the indices of the extreme prefixes to recover one optimal slice, ordering the two indices before slicing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Track maximum and minimum subarray sums separately with two Kadane-style scans and compare their magnitudes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:1074":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Count the nonempty axis-aligned rectangular regions of an integer matrix whose entries sum to a specified target. Rectangles are distinguished by their row and column boundaries, even when their contents happen to be equal.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The matrix is nonempty and rectangular. Values and the target may be negative, zero, or positive, so extending a rectangle does not move its sum in a predictable direction. A rectangle has four boundaries; testing all boundary quadruples is expensive. The two dimensions need not be equal, so choosing the smaller dimension for the pair of outer boundaries matters.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The returned count may be large: an all-zero matrix with target zero qualifies at every possible rectangle. Python integers accommodate this naturally, while implementations in fixed-width languages should check the required numeric range. The matrix is read only; no modification or square-grid assumption is needed.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Count all submatrices whose sum equals a target, in a grid of up to about 100 x 100.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fix the top and bottom rows of the submatrix. What 1D problem is left?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> 2D prefix sums\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each pair of rows (top, bottom), keep running column sums for the band; the submatrices inside that band with sum target are exactly the subarrays of the column-sum array with sum target, which the prefix-map count finds in O(columns). Put the smaller dimension in the outer pair loop.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(min(m, n)^2 x max(m, n)) time, O(max(m, n)) extra space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Choose top, bottom, left, and right boundaries, and add every cell in each chosen rectangle. This is O(m³n³) in the worst case, with O(1) auxiliary space if cells are traversed directly. A two-dimensional prefix table would reduce the rectangle sum to O(1), but still leaves O(m²n²) boundary choices and O(mn) table space. Either formulation is useful as an independent oracle on very small grids.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Fix two boundaries along one dimension. Collapse all cells between those boundaries into a one-dimensional array: each entry is the sum along the fixed band at one coordinate of the other dimension. Every rectangle inside that band corresponds to exactly one nonempty contiguous slice of this array, and its sum equals the slice sum. The two remaining rectangle boundaries are precisely that slice's endpoints.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For each fixed starting boundary, begin with a zeroed collapsed array and extend the ending boundary one step at a time, adding the newly included row or column. Count target-sum slices in the current array using prefix frequencies: if the current prefix is \u003ccode>s\u003c/code>, a previous prefix \u003ccode>s - target\u003c/code> creates a target-sum slice. Seed the empty prefix and query its frequency before recording the current prefix. Reinitialize this frequency map for every band; prefix positions from different bands must not mix.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Pair boundaries on the smaller dimension. The implementation uses a coordinate choice instead of transposing and copying the matrix: when rows are smaller it reads rows as bands, and otherwise it reads columns as bands. In either orientation the collapsed array has length equal to the larger dimension. This gives the advertised bound even for a very narrow matrix.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For correctness, each outer pair is visited exactly once. Within that pair each valid slice has a unique ending boundary, and the frequency lookup counts exactly its earlier starting boundary. Conversely, every counted pair of prefixes determines a nonempty slice and hence a valid rectangle with the chosen band. These mappings are bijective, so no rectangle is missed or counted twice.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">matrix = [[1, -1], [0, 1]], target = 0; pair rows\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">top=0, bottom=0: collapsed=[1, -1]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefixes 0, 1, 0: the last 0 matches the initial 0; add 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">top=0, bottom=1: collapsed=[1, 0]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefixes 0, 1, 1: the last 1 matches the earlier 1; add 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">top=1, bottom=1: collapsed=[0, 1]\u003c/p>\u003cpre>prefixes 0, 0, 1: the first new 0 matches the initial 0; add 1\u003c/pre>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">return 3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from collections import defaultdict\n\nclass Solution:\n    def numSubmatrixSumTarget(self, matrix, target):\n        m, n = len(matrix), len(matrix[0])\n        small, large = min(m, n), max(m, n)\n        pair_rows = m &lt;= n\n        answer = 0\n        for start in range(small):\n            collapsed = [0] * large\n            for end in range(start, small):\n                for j in range(large):\n                    collapsed[j] += matrix[end][j] if pair_rows else matrix[j][end]\n                seen = defaultdict(int)\n                seen[0] = 1\n                prefix = 0\n                for value in collapsed:\n                    prefix += value\n                    answer += seen[prefix - target]\n                    seen[prefix] += 1\n        return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>There are O(s²) bands for \u003ccode>s = min(m, n)\u003c/code>. Updating and counting each band costs expected O(l) with \u003ccode>l = max(m, n)\u003c/code> using a hash map. Total expected time is O(s²l). The collapsed array and its prefix-frequency map use O(l) auxiliary space; the input matrix is not copied.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Forgetting the empty prefix misses slices beginning at the first collapsed coordinate. Inserting a prefix before querying counts an empty slice when the target is zero. Reusing the frequency table across different bands incorrectly combines unrelated prefix sums. With a one-row or one-column matrix, there is only one possible band in the smaller dimension and the procedure reduces to the one-dimensional problem.\u003c/p>\u003cp>A zero target with repeated prefix sums requires frequencies rather than a set. Negative entries make a shrinking sliding window unreliable. If using a physically transposed copy instead of coordinate selection, include that copy in the space analysis.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Replace the frequency count with an earliest-prefix lookup to seek the largest-area target-sum rectangle, while retaining the fixed-band reduction.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a binary matrix and target equal to the area, investigate specialized all-ones rectangle methods instead of treating every sum independently.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:2536":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start with an \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> by \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> zero grid. Each query describes an inclusive rectangle by its top-left and bottom-right coordinates; add one to every cell in that rectangle. Return the resulting grid after all queries.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Coordinates are zero based, valid, and ordered. Queries may overlap, be identical, cover a single cell, or reach an edge. Only the final grid is requested, so individual updates need not be applied immediately. The output itself contains n² cells, establishing an unavoidable O(n²) output cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Add 1 to many sub-rectangles of an n x n grid, then return the whole grid.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The 1D trick marks a start and a stop. What marks does a rectangle need so that a 2D running sum recreates it?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Difference array\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use a 2D difference array: +1 at (r1, c1), -1 at (r1, c2 + 1), -1 at (r2 + 1, c1) and +1 at (r2 + 1, c2 + 1) in an (n + 1) x (n + 1) table, then take running sums along each row and then down each column.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(q + n^2) time, O(n^2) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>For each query loop over all its rows and columns, incrementing the affected cells. With \u003ccode>q\u003c/code> queries this costs O(n² + sum of rectangle areas), at most O(qn² + n²), and O(n²) space for the result. It directly models the operation and makes a good oracle for small grids.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A one-dimensional range increment can be expressed by a positive change at its start and an opposite change after its end. In two dimensions, mark four corners of a padded difference table: add at the top-left, subtract just to the right and just below the rectangle, and add back at the diagonally opposite corner. The last mark corrects the overlap of the two subtractions.\u003c/p>\u003cp>After marking all queries, prefix-sum the table across rows and then down columns. A cell sees a particular query's four marks exactly when their coordinates are above and to its left. Inclusion-exclusion makes the net contribution one inside that query and zero outside. Adding contributions across queries works because prefix sums are linear. A sentinel row and column allow the stop coordinates to be \u003ccode>n\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">n=3, query=[0, 1, 1, 2]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">mark +1 at (0,1), -1 at (0,3), -1 at (2,1), +1 at (2,3)\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">after row prefixes, rows 0 and 1 are [0,1,1] and [0,0,0] within the grid\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">after column prefixes, output rows are [0,1,1], [0,1,1], [0,0,0]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">sentinel column 3 carries the right stop and is not returned\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Solution:\n    def rangeAddQueries(self, n, queries):\n        diff = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n        for r1, c1, r2, c2 in queries:\n            diff[r1][c1] += 1\n            diff[r1][c2 + 1] -= 1\n            diff[r2 + 1][c1] -= 1\n            diff[r2 + 1][c2 + 1] += 1\n        for r in range(n):\n            for c in range(1, n):\n                diff[r][c] += diff[r][c - 1]\n        for r in range(1, n):\n            for c in range(n):\n                diff[r][c] += diff[r - 1][c]\n        return [row[:n] for row in diff[:n]]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Marking queries takes O(q) time; the two prefix passes and output copy take O(n²), for O(q + n²) total time. The padded table and returned grid each occupy O(n²) space.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The diagonal correction must be positive, not negative. An inclusive bottom or right edge uses a stop one position later. Do not construct the table by multiplying one inner list, which would alias rows. Sentinel cells need not be integrated because they lie outside every returned coordinate. Empty queries produce an all-zero grid.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Give each query its own signed increment by replacing the four unit marks with that increment and its negation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Support a rectangular \u003ccode>m\u003c/code> by \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> grid by padding and scanning each dimension separately.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:995":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Given a binary array and a positive window length \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>, a move toggles every bit in one contiguous window of exactly that length. Return the smallest number of moves that makes every bit one, or -1 if this cannot be done. A toggle changes zero to one and one to zero.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The array contains only zeroes and ones, and \u003ccode>1 &lt;= k &lt;= n\u003c/code>. A move may start only where its entire window fits. Windows may overlap, so a bit can be toggled several times; only the parity of that count affects its final value. A naive implementation that toggles each of \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> bits per chosen move can take O(nk), which is unnecessary when windows are long.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The procedure below does not change the supplied array. Its boundary table is separate, and therefore the solution can be called again with the same input. A window of length one and a window covering the entire array are both within the contract and exercise different boundary cases.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Flip windows of exactly k consecutive bits to turn the whole array into 1s, with as few flips as possible.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Look at the leftmost 0. Which flips can still change it, and is there any choice about using one?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Difference array\u003c/p>\u003cp>Scan left to right keeping the number of active flips with a difference array (+1 where a flip starts, -1 at start + k). If the bit at i is 0 after an even number of flips (or 1 after an odd number), a flip must start at i, since no later flip covers i; if i + k &gt; n, return -1. Each step is O(1).\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space (O(1) with in-place markers)\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>One direct small-input oracle is to enumerate subsets of all possible window starts, apply each selected window to a copy, and choose the fewest selected windows that make the copy all ones. There are \u003ccode>n-k+1\u003c/code> possible starts, so at most O(2^(n-k+1) nk) work with a direct simulation and O(n) working space. Considering each start at most once is sufficient because two identical toggles cancel; removing both never worsens the move count. A breadth-first search over bit arrays gives another independent baseline.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Scan from left to right. When position \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> is reached, all decisions at earlier starts have been made, and no future start greater than \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> can affect position \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>. If its effective bit is zero, a flip starting exactly at \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> is forced in any completion of those decisions. If its effective bit is one, starting a flip here would make it zero permanently, so no flip is allowed at this start. If a forced window would extend past the array, the instance is impossible.\u003c/p>\u003cp>This is a proof by induction over positions. Initially there are no earlier decisions. Assuming every feasible solution agrees with the decisions before \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>, the final value at \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> forces the next decision as described. Thus the algorithm either detects an impossibility shared by all solutions or produces the only useful choice at each start. The resulting valid sequence is optimal: repeating the same window twice would cancel and can never improve a minimum solution.\u003c/p>\u003cp>To avoid toggling an entire window, maintain the parity of flips currently covering \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>. A difference array records where each chosen flip expires: toggle a marker at \u003ccode>i+k\u003c/code>. At each position first apply its expiration marker, then compute the effective bit as the original bit XOR the active parity. When that bit is zero, toggle the active parity, record its future expiration, and increase the answer. A sentinel at \u003ccode>n\u003c/code> safely handles windows ending at the last element.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The loop invariant is that before testing the effective bit, active parity equals the number of earlier chosen windows still covering this index modulo two. Expiration happens before the test because a length-k window beginning at \u003ccode>s\u003c/code> covers only \u003ccode>s\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>s+k-1\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>s+k\u003c/code>. This representation yields the same effective bits as actually applying all chosen windows.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums=[0, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0], k=3; active=0, flips=0\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=0: effective 0, flip [0..2], active=1, expire at 3, flips=1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=1: effective 1; i=2: effective 1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=3: expire to active=0; effective 1, no flip\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=4: effective 0, flip [4..6], active=1, expire at 7, flips=2\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=5: effective 0, flip [5..7], active=0, expire at 8, flips=3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">i=6: effective 1; i=7: expire to active=1, effective 1\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">return 3\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Solution:\n    def minKBitFlips(self, nums, k):\n        n = len(nums)\n        expires = [0] * (n + 1)\n        active = answer = 0\n        for i, bit in enumerate(nums):\n            active ^= expires[i]\n            if bit ^ active == 0:\n                if i + k &gt; n:\n                    return -1\n                answer += 1\n                active ^= 1\n                expires[i + k] ^= 1\n        return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The scan does O(1) work per bit, so time is O(n). The expiration table takes O(n) auxiliary space. A mutation-based encoding can reduce auxiliary space, but then the input contract and cleanup become important.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Expiration is at \u003ccode>i+k\u003c/code>, not the last included index \u003ccode>i+k-1\u003c/code>. Check whether the window fits before recording it. An already all-one array requires zero moves, while a late effective zero with no room to start a window requires -1. Overlapping flips cancel in parity; counting only the most recent flip fails. Use XOR or counts modulo two consistently.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the start indices as well as the count by appending \u003ccode>i\u003c/code> whenever a forced flip is made.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If changing window lengths are allowed, the forced-decision argument no longer fixes a unique action; formulate a different optimization rather than reusing this greedy proof.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>","p:689":"\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>The problem\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Select exactly three non-overlapping contiguous windows of length \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> from an integer array. Maximize their combined sum, and among equally good choices return the lexicographically smallest triple of starting indices in increasing order.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Constraints and what they imply\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The input admits three windows: \u003ccode>k &gt;= 1\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>len(nums) &gt;= 3*k\u003c/code>. In the standard setting values are positive, but the code also works with negative values. Windows of equal length do not overlap exactly when consecutive starts differ by at least \u003ccode>k\u003c/code>. A tie must be resolved on the full ordered triple, not merely on its total or its last index.\u003c/p>\u003cp>There are O(n) possible starts for a single window. Three nested choices have cubic worst-case cost, even if each window sum is available in constant time. The key is to fix the middle window: after it is fixed, the legal left and right choices lie in separate ranges and can be optimized independently.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"hn\">\u003cp class=\"hl\">Stuck? Open the hints one at a time:\u003c/p>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 1: what to notice\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Choose three non-overlapping length-k subarrays with the largest total, preferring the smallest starting indices on ties.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 2: a nudge\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Fix the middle window. What do you need to know about the best window entirely to its left and the best entirely to its right?\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003cdetails class=\"hs\">\u003csummary>Hint 3: the key insight\u003c/summary>\u003cp>\u003cb>Pattern:\u003c/b> Prefix and suffix passes\u003c/p>\u003cp>Compute every window sum from prefix sums, then best_left[i] (index of the best window starting at or before i, keeping the earlier one on ties with &gt;) and best_right[i] (best starting at or after i, preferring the earlier one with &gt;= while scanning right to left). Try every middle start and combine three O(1) lookups.\u003c/p>\u003cp class=\"cx\">\u003cb>Target:\u003c/b> O(n) time, O(n) space\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdetails class=\"sol\">\u003csummary>Show the solution: brute force, insight, traced optimal, code\u003c/summary>\u003cdiv class=\"pc\">\u003ch4>Brute force\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Enumerate every triple of starts \u003ccode>a &lt; b &lt; c\u003c/code> with \u003ccode>a+k &lt;= b\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>b+k &lt;= c\u003c/code>. Sum the three windows and keep the greatest total, breaking ties lexicographically. Directly summing the windows costs O(n³k) in the worst case; precomputing window sums reduces this to O(n³ + n) time and O(n) space. A direct triple enumeration is particularly useful as an oracle for small inputs and negative values.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>The insight\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First compute all length-k window sums with a rolling sum. Let \u003ccode>w[i]\u003c/code> be the sum of the window starting at \u003ccode>i\u003c/code>, with \u003ccode>L = n-k+1\u003c/code> possible starts. Build \u003ccode>left[i]\u003c/code>, the best starting index among \u003ccode>0..i\u003c/code>; when two sums tie, retain the earlier index. Build \u003ccode>right[i]\u003c/code>, the best starting index among \u003ccode>i..L-1\u003c/code>; while scanning from right to left, replace on equality so the newly encountered, smaller index wins.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For a fixed middle start \u003ccode>b\u003c/code>, the best legal left start is \u003ccode>left[b-k]\u003c/code>, and the best legal right start is \u003ccode>right[b+k]\u003c/code>. These ranges are independent: any left candidate ends before \u003ccode>b\u003c/code>, and any right candidate begins after the middle window. Maximizing each side separately therefore maximizes the combined total for that middle. The earliest best index on each side also gives the lexicographically smallest triple for that particular middle.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Enumerate middle starts from \u003ccode>k\u003c/code> through \u003ccode>L-k-1\u003c/code>, inclusive. Compare the candidate triples first by total and then explicitly by lexicographic order. This explicit comparison enforces the global tie rule without relying on scan order. Initialize the best total with \u003ccode>None\u003c/code>, rather than zero, so an all-negative extension of the input contract still works.\u003c/p>\u003cp>For correctness, each valid triple has exactly one middle start in the enumeration. For that start, replacing either outer window by its precomputed best cannot reduce its sum and preserves disjointness. Among equal-sum replacements the stored outer indices are earliest. Consequently the selected candidate is at least as good as any triple with the same middle, including its tie order. Comparing all candidates gives the global optimum and its lexicographically smallest representative.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Optimal solution, traced\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"trace\">\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">nums=[1, 2, 1, 2, 1, 2], k=2; window sums=[3, 3, 3, 3, 3]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">left=[0, 0, 0, 0, 0]; right=[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">only middle start b=2 is legal\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">left[0]=0, middle=2, right[4]=4; total=9\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cp class=\"sc\">return [0, 2, 4]\u003c/p>\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Code\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>class Solution:\n    def maxSumOfThreeSubarrays(self, nums, k):\n        n = len(nums)\n        window = sum(nums[:k])\n        sums = [window]\n        for i in range(k, n):\n            window += nums[i] - nums[i - k]\n            sums.append(window)\n        count = len(sums)\n        left = [0] * count\n        best = 0\n        for i in range(count):\n            if sums[i] &gt; sums[best]:\n                best = i\n            left[i] = best\n        right = [0] * count\n        best = count - 1\n        for i in range(count - 1, -1, -1):\n            if sums[i] &gt;= sums[best]:\n                best = i\n            right[i] = best\n        best_total = None\n        answer = None\n        for middle in range(k, count - k):\n            a, c = left[middle - k], right[middle + k]\n            total = sums[a] + sums[middle] + sums[c]\n            candidate = [a, middle, c]\n            if best_total is None or total &gt; best_total or (total == best_total and candidate &lt; answer):\n                best_total, answer = total, candidate\n        return answer\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Complexity\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The rolling sums, two best-index arrays, and middle scan each take O(n) time. The three arrays use O(n) auxiliary space. Python's constant-size triple comparison is O(1).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mistakes and edge cases\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Using \u003ccode>&gt;=\u003c/code> in the left-to-right pass overwrites an earlier tied left index; using \u003ccode>&gt;\u003c/code> in the right-to-left pass keeps a later tied right index. The right boundary is \u003ccode>middle+k\u003c/code>, not \u003ccode>middle+k-1\u003c/code>, because windows must not overlap. The smallest valid array has exactly one triple. Many equal windows exercise tie rules, while negative test values expose an incorrectly zero-initialized optimum.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Variations\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Return the maximum total instead of indices by keeping only the best candidate's score.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For a general number of windows, consider dynamic programming over the number chosen and the allowed prefix, with explicit tie handling.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003c/div>\u003c/details>"}]);
